 #ifdef __vita__
         /*
-         * VitaSurf: the string this device's own browser sends.
+         * VitaSurf: what this is, on what, and that it is a mobile device.
          *
-         * A site that decides what to serve by reading this had no rule
-         * for "PlayStation Vita; Mobile", which is a string nobody has
-         * ever seen, so several of them fell back to their full desktop
-         * application. Measured against YouTube: the old string was
-         * served www.youtube.com with 10.8 MB of script files, and this
-         * one is served m.youtube.com with 1.8 MB. The device spent
-         * twenty-six seconds compiling the difference. Wikipedia serves
-         * the same bytes either way, so this is about being recognised,
-         * not about any one site.
-         *
-         * It claims AppleWebKit and Safari, which we are not, as every
-         * browser's string does; what makes it the right one rather than
-         * a convenient one is that it is what this hardware sends, so
-         * the pages it asks for are pages meant for this screen and this
-         * processor. An old WebKit also means conservative JavaScript,
-         * which suits an interpreter with no JIT.
-         *
-         * The Mobile token is kept from the old string. The device's own
-         * string does not carry it, and plenty of sites look for exactly
-         * that word rather than for a device they know; measured across
-         * six sites, adding it changed nothing that worked without it
-         * and covers the ones that were never asked.
+         * The string was the Vita's own browser's, a 2012 WebKit (Silk
+         * 3.2), chosen so sites that read it would serve their mobile
+         * pages: with "PlayStation Vita; Mobile" alone YouTube served
+         * www.youtube.com, 10.8 MB of script, and with the Silk string
+         * m.youtube.com, 1.8 MB. But a browser claiming a 2012 WebKit
+         * while running WebAssembly, shadow DOM and every Intl API is a
+         * contradiction a site can see, and Cloudflare's challenges give
+         * browsers more than five years old only limited support; on
+         * claude.ai the check passed and the clearance it set was
+         * refused at once. So the string now names the engine and the
+         * device as they are, and keeps the Mobile token both where
+         * Firefox for Android has it and at the end, where the sites
+         * that look for "Mobi" or "Mobile" find it.
          */
-        {
